ssh: and telnet: links: refuse a user or host that is not a plain name.

A link's user and host became arguments of ssh or telnet as they were, so
ssh://u@h%20-oProxyCommand=... ran "ssh -l u h -oProxyCommand=...": code
execution from a link, through a profile configured as the scheme's handler.
Backported from upstream 168d0eee0f (host) and ad73877882 (user), with the
leading-character check upstream's sanitizedHostname adds later: a host must
start with a letter or digit and hold only letters, digits, ":", "-" and ".";
a user only letters, digits, "_", "-", "+" and ".", and, beyond upstream, not
start with "-". Either is shell-escaped as upstream does. A refused link opens
nothing (launchBookmark returns nil, as upstream's does).

A handler profile with a custom command takes the link's parts as $$HOST$$,
$$USER$$, $$URL$$, $$PATH$$, $$RES$$ and $$PASSWORD$$, then splits the command
into arguments. The same checks hold there (iTermURLSubstitutions): a user or
host that is not a plain name opens no session, and every free-form part is
shell-escaped so it stays one argument.

